WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.260
Authorization answers a different question from authentication: is this identity allowed to perform this action on this resource right now.

2
00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:12.360
That decision belongs on the server, close to the operation being protected.

3
00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:20.780
Hidden buttons, client-side routes, and user interface conditions can improve usability, but they are not sufficient access control.

4
00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:29.060
ASP.NET Core supports roles, policies, claims, requirements, handlers, and endpoint-level authorization.

5
00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:41.320
Roles are useful when they match real responsibilities, but they can become too coarse when access depends on tenant, ownership, workflow state, data sensitivity, business unit, or operation type.

6
00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:45.580
Policy-based authorization gives teams a clearer place to express those rules.

7
00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:58.500
Apply authorization consistently across pages, controllers, minimal APIs, endpoint groups, background actions, and service-layer operations as appropriate for the application architecture.

8
00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:08.200
A protected controller action is not enough if the same behavior is reachable through a different endpoint, a background job, an export feature, or an administrative support tool.

9
00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:12.240
Object-level authorization is where many applications become fragile.

10
00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:16.340
A user may be allowed to open the reports feature, but not every report.

11
00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:20.360
A tenant administrator may manage users in one tenant, but not another.

12
00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:24.940
A service account may process one queue, but not update unrelated records.

13
00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:32.480
Access decisions should include the specific record, file, tenant, action, and current state when those factors matter.

14
00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:35.720
Least privilege applies beyond end users.

15
00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:47.020
Administrators, support staff, background services, database accounts, managed identities, deployment pipelines, and scheduled jobs should have only the permissions they need.

16
00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:57.360
Administrative and support workflows are high-risk paths because they often combine broad visibility, powerful actions, and pressure to resolve issues quickly.

17
00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:01.400
Scattered permission logic becomes inconsistent over time.

18
00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:10.700
Central policies, resource-based authorization handlers, well-named service methods, and consistent review patterns make access control easier to audit.

19
00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:17.920
A reviewer should be able to locate the decision, understand the data it uses, and confirm the same rule is applied wherever the operation can happen.
